//@ expect: accept
//@ reason: `data = bytes;` (a bare-name field store, implicit `this`) links/shares
//@ into a field -- it does NOT consume `bytes`, exactly like an object-typed
//@ bare field store (field_stores_owner_accepted.clg's precedent). Using `bytes`
//@ afterward is ordinary, safe reuse, not a use-after-move.
//@ run: 2
//
// Positive control for vector_moved_into_declaration_rejected.clg's move case
// and vector_moved_into_call_rejected.clg's call-argument move case: a
// container-typed field assignment must NOT be treated the same way. Found
// while fixing AstEmitter::emitMovedArg to recognize vector-typed identifiers
// (see that fixture's own reason): the first version of the fix moved
// EVERY container-typed identifier reachable from emitMovedArg, including
// through this exact field-assignment shape, which DanglingChecker's own
// isBareFieldStore handling has always treated as a link/share, not a move
// (see dangling_checker.cpp's AssignmentStatement branch) -- generating
// `this->data = std::move(bytes);` there compiled and ran, silently printing
// 0 instead of the real element count, with the checker never rejecting it
// (this is not a move-then-use gap a checker could catch after the fact --
// the checker's own model says `bytes` stays live, so the emitter breaking
// that promise is invisible to it). The //@ run: assertion below is the
// actual regression guard: it fails loudly (prints 0) if this ever regresses.

class Holder {
public:
    std::vector<int> data;

    void take(std::vector<int> bytes) {
        data = bytes;
        std::cout << bytes.size() << std::endl;
    }
};

int main() {
    Holder h = new Holder();
    std::vector<int> v;
    v.push_back(1);
    v.push_back(2);
    h.take(v);
    return 0;
}
